<section xml:id="Section-1">
 <title>Spanning and Existence of Solutions</title>

      <p>
        One property the vectors
        <me>\begin{pmatrix} 1\\0 \end{pmatrix} \text{ and } \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\1 \end{pmatrix}</me>
        have is that any two dimensional vector can be written as a linear combination of them. In notation,
        <me>\text{span}\left( \begin{pmatrix} 1\\0 \end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\1 \end{pmatrix}\right) = \mathbb{R}^{2}.</me>
        In other words, the linear equation with vector coefficients
        <me>x\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\0\end{pmatrix} + y \begin{pmatrix} 0\\1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} b_{1}\\b_{2} \end{pmatrix}</me>
        always has a solution. In matrix form
        <me>\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 0 \\0 \amp1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} x\\y \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} b_{1} \\ b_{2} \end{pmatrix}</me>
      </p>

      <p>We call such vectors <alert>spanning</alert>. Today, our first goal is to characterize when a set of vectors is spanning. It turns out by making those vectors the columns of a matrix <m>A</m>, this can be entirely determined from the matrix.</p>

      <theorem>
        <statement>
          <p>A collection of <m>n</m> vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^{m}</m> is spanning if in reduced row echelon form of the <m>m \times n</m> 
          matrix with those columns as vectors has <m>m</m> pivot columns.</p>
        </statement>
      </theorem>

      <p>
        For example, <m>\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\0 \\0\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\begin{pmatrix} 1\\1\\ 0\end{pmatrix}</m> are not spanning. If you put them as the columns of a matrix, you obtain
        <me>\begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\0 \amp1\\0 \amp0\end{pmatrix}</me>
        Doing replacement on the first row by subtracting the second yields the reduced row echelon form:
        <me>\begin{pmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\0 \amp1\\0 \amp0\end{pmatrix}</me>
        There are only <m>2</m> pivot columns and the vectors have <m>3</m> entries, so they are not spanning.
      </p>

      <p>The essential reason for this is the number of pivot columns is the number of rows with nonzero entries in reduced row echelon form. 
      If there are fewer than <m>m</m> pivot columns, then the bottom row has to be a row of all <m>0</m>'s
       That means if the righthand side has a <m>1</m> in the last coordinate, you get the equation <m>0 = 1</m>, which has no solution.</p>

      <p>Reinterpreting this in terms of spans, we find.</p>

      <theorem>
        <statement>
          <p>If a collection of <m>n</m> vectors spans <m>\mathbb{R}^{m}</m>, then <m>n \geq m</m>.</p>
        </statement>
      </theorem>

      <p>Why? This is because the matrix with them has at least <m>m</m> pivot columns, meaning it has to have at least <m>m</m> columns, 
      and <m>n</m> is the number of columns. Therefore, <m>n \geq m</m>.</p>
    </section>